1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:05,568
This video shows you how to catch an exception in your code,

2
00:00:05,568 --> 00:00:08,190
and decide what happens to the record.

3
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:10,967
Here's the Code step from the last video.

4
00:00:10,967 --> 00:00:12,710
Two records still fail.

5
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:16,463
Their lines are stack traces, so json can't read them.

6
00:00:16,463 --> 00:00:20,150
Each record is marked with an Unparsable exception.

7
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:24,452
A marked record goes to its exception answer at the end of the pipeline.

8
00:00:24,452 --> 00:00:27,347
But a stack trace is a normal line from this app.

9
00:00:27,347 --> 00:00:28,950
It shouldn't be marked.

10
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,044
Put the json call in a try block.

11
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:38,230
A catch block runs on each record that failed in the try.

12
00:00:38,230 --> 00:00:40,110
e holds the exception.

13
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:42,275
Record what went wrong.

14
00:00:42,275 --> 00:00:45,390
e.kind is the kind of exception.

15
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:47,104
Now no record fails.

16
00:00:47,104 --> 00:00:49,950
The catch cleared the exception.

17
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,215
The stack trace keeps its message, and app_error says Unparsable.

18
00:00:56,280 --> 00:01:01,445
The records that parsed never reach the catch, so they have no app_error.

19
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:04,848
Save.

20
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:09,295
Check on learning.

21
00:01:09,495 --> 00:01:13,114
Which records does a catch block run on?

22
00:01:17,514 --> 00:01:21,429
The records that failed inside its try.

23
00:01:22,329 --> 00:01:26,643
What happens to an exception that a catch block catches?

24
00:01:31,043 --> 00:01:35,158
It's cleared, so the record is no longer marked.

25
00:01:36,058 --> 00:01:38,891
Next, you work with arrays.
